exports[`M15 deterministic prompt generation > snapshot 'sticker-badge/custom/detailed' 1`] = `
"## Objective
Implement the configured StickerBadge integration. Label, select, and remove with native semantics.

## Package and installation
Use StickerBadge from the public @scout-ui/react package and import @scout-ui/react/styles.css once at the existing application style boundary.
Inspect the manifest and current package manager; add @scout-ui/react only if it is absent, without claiming an unpublished version.

## Project inspection
Inspect the existing React entry, component structure, styling convention, package manager, and test setup before editing files.

## Exact selected configuration
- Mode: select. Choose static label, selection, or whole-badge removal semantics.
- Label: "Fresh signal". Set the compact visible label.
- Tone: cyan. Choose an expressive semantic tone.
- Shape: stamp. Use label, stamp, or a true pill.
- Size: default. Choose compact, default, or large target geometry.
- Rotation: -1. Keep compact label rotation within a restrained range.
- Selected: enabled. Set the pressed selection state.

## Integration and layout
Inspect the existing page and component hierarchy, then choose the smallest appropriate integration point for StickerBadge; do not invent a filename.
Preserve the existing layout and unrelated visual structure, integrate at the smallest necessary boundary, and do not rewrite unrelated application logic.
Inspect the project's existing asset convention first, then use public, local, or safely referenced remote artwork without scraping or copying protected assets.
No repository context was supplied. Inspect the project and ask for clarification rather than asserting unknown project facts.

## Accessibility and reduced motion
Each mode maps to its frozen native element contract.
Selection uses aria-pressed and a visible check.
Render static mode as a span, select mode as one button with aria-pressed, and remove mode as one named button with no nested interactive control.
Communicate selection with more than color, retain visible focus and target size, and keep keyboard behavior native.
If selection and removal are both needed, compose sibling controls with group semantics rather than inventing a hybrid badge.

## SSR, interaction, cleanup, and performance
Keep the server-compatible leaf safe for static rendering; interactive callbacks belong below the consumer's smallest Client Component boundary.

## Verification and non-regression
Verify the configured native element, accessible name and state, keyboard activation, focus visibility, selection signal, disabled behavior where used, SSR, and absence of nested interaction.
Run the project's existing formatting, lint, type, unit, browser, accessibility, and build checks that cover the changed integration; fix failures rather than weakening tests.
"
`;

