"## Objective
Implement the configured StickerButton integration. Give actions and links physical press feedback.

## Package and installation
Use StickerButton from the public @scout-ui/react package and import @scout-ui/react/styles.css once at the existing application style boundary.
Inspect the manifest and current package manager; add @scout-ui/react only if it is absent, without claiming an unpublished version.

## Project inspection
Inspect the existing React entry, component structure, styling convention, package manager, and test setup before editing files.

## Exact selected configuration
- Element: Link. Choose native button or anchor semantics.
- Label: "Make it stick". Set the visible action label.
- Tone: cyan. Choose the action surface tone.
- Size: default. Choose compact, default, or large target geometry.
- Shape: paper. Use label, paper, or pill when semantically appropriate.
- Leading mark: disabled. Show authored leading content.
- Trailing arrow: enabled. Show authored trailing content.
- Full width: disabled. Fill the available preview row.
- Motion policy: System. Use system motion preference or always choose static feedback.

## Integration and layout
Inspect the existing page and component hierarchy, then choose the smallest appropriate integration point for StickerButton; do not invent a filename.
Preserve the existing layout and unrelated visual structure, integrate at the smallest necessary boundary, and do not rewrite unrelated application logic.
Inspect the project's existing asset convention first, then use public, local, or safely referenced remote artwork without scraping or copying protected assets.
No repository context was supplied. Inspect the project and ask for clarification rather than asserting unknown project facts.

## Accessibility and reduced motion
Links remain anchors; actions remain buttons.
Always preserves color, outline, and depth feedback without translation.
Use a native button for actions and a real anchor for navigation; never place interactive descendants inside the control.
Loading belongs only to the button branch, preserves dimensions, exposes busy context, and prevents duplicate activation.
Retain visible focus, at least 44px target geometry, contrast, disabled semantics, and reduced-motion feedback without required translation.

## SSR, interaction, cleanup, and performance
Keep the server-compatible leaf safe for static and anchor rendering; event callbacks belong under the consumer's smallest client boundary.

## Verification and non-regression
Verify the configured native element, normal link behavior or button activation, loading/disabled guards, focus, target size, reduced motion, SSR, and no layout shift.
Run the project's existing formatting, lint, type, unit, browser, accessibility, and build checks that cover the changed integration; fix failures rather than weakening tests.
"
`;

